sections.body:
You'll often want a prop to have a sensible fallback when the parent doesn't bother passing it. Because props arrive as a single destructured parameter object, you can lean on ordinary JavaScript default-parameter syntax:

```jsx
function Button({ label, variant = "primary" }) {
  return <button className={variant}>{label}</button>;
}

<Button label="Save" />                 // variant defaults to "primary"
<Button label="Delete" variant="danger" /> // variant is explicitly "danger"
```

The detail worth internalizing precisely: a default value only kicks in when the prop is `undefined` -- that is, when the parent genuinely didn't pass it at all, or explicitly passed `variant={undefined}`. It does **not** kick in for other falsy values. `<Button label="Save" variant={null} />` or `<Button label="Save" variant={0} />` will NOT fall back to `"primary"` -- the component receives exactly `null` or `0`, because JavaScript's default-parameter mechanism specifically checks for `undefined`, nothing broader. This is a common source of confusion for anyone expecting "falsy means use the default"; the actual rule is narrower and worth remembering exactly.

`children` is not special React syntax -- it's an entirely ordinary prop that React populates automatically with whatever you nest between a component's opening and closing JSX tags:

```jsx
function Card({ title, children }) {
  return (
    <div className="card">
      <h3>{title}</h3>
      <div className="card-body">{children}</div>
    </div>
  );
}

<Card title="Profile">
  <p>Name: Amara</p>
  <button>Edit</button>
</Card>
```

Everything between `<Card ...>` and `</Card>` -- the paragraph and the button -- becomes `props.children` inside `Card`, without `Card` ever needing to know or care what's actually inside. This is what makes **composition** possible: `Card` only needs to know where to place its content (wrapped in a title and a bordered box); the caller decides what that content actually is, every time, without `Card` needing a separate prop for every possible thing someone might want to put inside it. This same pattern is how layout components, modals, and wrappers of every kind are built in React -- accept `children`, decide how to arrange it, and let the composition happen at the call site.

Since data only flows one way -- down -- a child can never reach up and directly change something the parent owns. So how does a child report that something happened (a button was clicked, a value was typed) if it can't touch the parent's data itself? By calling a **function the parent handed it as a prop**. This is the standard, and really the *only*, way a child communicates upward in React:

```jsx
function LikeButton({ isLiked, onToggle }) {
  return (
    <button onClick={onToggle}>
      {isLiked ? "\u2665 Liked" : "\u2661 Like"}
    </button>
  );
}

function Post({ post, onLikeToggle }) {
  return (
    <article>
      <p>{post.text}</p>
      <LikeButton isLiked={post.liked} onToggle={() => onLikeToggle(post.id)} />
    </article>
  );
}
```

`LikeButton` never touches `post` or any state directly -- it just calls `onToggle` (a function it received) when clicked. The parent (`Post`, or further up, whoever actually owns the `liked` state) decides what `onLikeToggle` does, typically calling a state setter. The naming convention `onX` for a callback prop (`onToggle`, `onLikeToggle`, `onClick`) versus `handleX` for the function that actually implements the response (`handleLikeToggle`) is worth adopting early -- it makes the direction of communication obvious at a glance: `onX` is what a child is given to call; `handleX` is the parent's own implementation of what happens when it's called.

examples.code:
// ANTI-PATTERN: reassigning a prop inside the component
function BrokenPriceTag({ amount }) {
  amount = amount * 1.1; // adding 10% tax -- looks harmless, isn't
  return <span>${amount.toFixed(2)}</span>;
}

// CORRECT: derive a new local value, leave the prop untouched
function PriceTag({ amount }) {
  const amountWithTax = amount * 1.1;
  return <span>${amountWithTax.toFixed(2)}</span>;
}

function Card({ title, children }) {
  return (
    <div className="card">
      <h3>{title}</h3>
      <div className="card-body">{children}</div>
    </div>
  );
}

<Card title="Profile">
  <p>Name: Amara</p>
  <button>Edit</button>
</Card>

<Card title="Empty State">
  <p>No results found.</p>
</Card>

