exercises.solutionCode:
function ShoppingCartItem({ item, onApplyDiscount }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <span>{item.name}: ${item.price.toFixed(2)}</span>
      <button onClick={() => onApplyDiscount(item.id)}>Apply 10% off</button>
    </div>
  );
}

// The parent owns the cart data and is the only one allowed to change it:
function ShoppingCart({ items, onApplyDiscount }) {
  return (
    <div>
      {items.map((item) => (
        <ShoppingCartItem key={item.id} item={item} onApplyDiscount={onApplyDiscount} />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}

sections.body:
Props and state are both just data a component has access to while rendering, but they differ in one crucial way: **who owns them, and who's allowed to change them.** A prop is external input, owned by the parent -- the component receiving it only ever reads it (as the previous concept covered). **State** is the opposite: it's data the component owns itself, can change over the course of its lifetime in response to things like user interaction, and -- critically -- changing it is what causes the component to re-render with new output.

In a function component, state is introduced with the `useState` hook (covered in full depth later in this course; here you only need the basic shape):

```jsx
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Clicked {count} times</button>;
}
```

`useState(0)` returns a pair: the current value (`count`) and a function to update it (`setCount`). Calling `setCount(...)` does two things at once: it stores the new value for this component instance, and it tells React to re-render this component so its output reflects that new value. Nothing about props works this way -- a prop only ever changes because the *parent* re-rendered and passed a new value down; the child has no setter for its own props at all.

This gets more deceptive with objects and arrays, because mutating one of those really does change the underlying data -- it's just that React still never finds out:

```jsx
function ProfileForm() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState({ name: "Amara", age: 29 });

  function haveBirthday() {
    user.age = user.age + 1; // mutates the object in place -- doesn't work
    console.log(user.age);   // logs the new age just fine!
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <p>{user.name} is {user.age}</p>
      <button onClick={haveBirthday}>Happy birthday</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

`user.age = user.age + 1` genuinely mutates the object React is holding in state -- `console.log(user.age)` proves the new value really exists. But React doesn't inspect the internals of whatever value it's storing to detect that something inside it changed; it only reacts to `setUser` being called. So the component never re-renders, and the paragraph keeps showing the old age indefinitely, even though the actual object backing it has already changed. The fix is to never mutate the existing object, and instead give `setUser` a brand new one: `setUser({ ...user, age: user.age + 1 })`. This both updates the stored value and, because it's an actual call to the setter, correctly triggers a re-render.

examples.code:
function BrokenCounter() {
  let count = 0;

  function handleClick() {
    count = count + 1;
    console.log(count);
  }

  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Clicked {count} times</button>;
}

function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  function handleClick() {
    setCount(count + 1);
  }

  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Clicked {count} times</button>;
}

function ProfileForm() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState({ name: "Amara", age: 29 });

  function haveBirthdayBroken() {
    user.age = user.age + 1;      // mutates the stored object -- no re-render
  }

  function haveBirthdayFixed() {
    setUser({ ...user, age: user.age + 1 }); // new object -- triggers re-render
  }

  return <p>{user.name} is {user.age}</p>;
}

