function TodoItem({ todo, todos, setTodos }) {
  function toggleDone() {
    todo.done = !todo.done;
    setTodos(todos);
  }

  return (
    <li onClick={toggleDone} style={{ textDecoration: todo.done ? "line-through" : "none" }}>
      {todo.text}
    </li>
  );
}

exercises.solutionCode:
function TodoItem({ todo, todos, setTodos }) {
  function toggleDone() {
    setTodos(
      todos.map((t) => (t.id === todo.id ? { ...t, done: !t.done } : t))
    );
  }

  return (
    <li onClick={toggleDone} style={{ textDecoration: todo.done ? "line-through" : "none" }}>
      {todo.text}
    </li>
  );
}

sections.body:
The standard React pattern for a text input is to make it **controlled**: the input's displayed value comes from state, and every keystroke updates that state via `onChange`.

```jsx
function NameField() {
  const [name, setName] = useState("");

  return (
    <input
      value={name}
      onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)}
      placeholder="Enter your name"
    />
  );
}
```

Trace the actual sequence on every keystroke: the user presses a key, the browser fires a native input event, React's `onChange` handler runs, you read the freshly typed value off `e.target.value`, you call `setName` with it, the component re-renders, and the input's `value` prop is set again -- this time to the value you just stored. Visually this feels instantaneous and invisible, but it's a real round trip every single keystroke, not the DOM independently tracking what was typed. The payoff for this indirection: at any given instant, the input's displayed content genuinely *is* your application state -- you can validate it, transform it, mirror it elsewhere in the UI, or disable a submit button based on it, all by reading that one piece of state, with no separate step required to 'ask the DOM what it currently contains.'

Forms are wired up with `<form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>` rather than listening for a click on the submit button directly, since `onSubmit` also fires correctly for other ways of submitting a form (like pressing Enter inside a text field).

The detail that trips up nearly everyone the first time: a browser's *native* behavior for submitting a form is to perform an actual page navigation -- reloading the current page or navigating to whatever URL the form's `action` points to (or the current page, by default). In a React single-page app, this is almost never what you want, since React is already handling the form's data entirely in state and doesn't need (or want) the browser to reload anything. So the very first line of a React `onSubmit` handler is almost always `e.preventDefault()`:

```jsx
function handleSubmit(e) {
  e.preventDefault();
  console.log("Submitting:", email, password);
}
```

Forgetting it doesn't throw an error or crash anything -- it silently lets the browser's default navigation proceed, which, for a same-page form, typically means a full page reload. That wipes out every piece of React state your app was holding and looks, from the user's perspective, like the page 'flashed' and reset for no clear reason, which is a genuinely confusing symptom to debug if you don't already know to expect it.

examples.code:
function NameField() {
  const [name, setName] = useState("");

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        value={name}
        onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)}
        placeholder="Enter your name"
      />
      <p>You typed: {name}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

function LoginForm() {
  const [email, setEmail] = useState("");
  const [password, setPassword] = useState("");

  function handleSubmit(e) {
    e.preventDefault();
    console.log("Logging in with", email, password);
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <input value={email} onChange={(e) => setEmail(e.target.value)} />
      <input type="password" value={password} onChange={(e) => setPassword(e.target.value)} />
      <button type="submit">Log In</button>
    </form>
  );
}

