sections.body:
`&&` short-circuits by returning whichever operand it actually lands on -- not necessarily a boolean. This becomes a real, subtle bug the moment the left-hand side can be `0`:

```jsx
function CartBadge({ itemCount }) {
  return <div>{itemCount && <span className="badge">{itemCount}</span>}</div>;
}
```

When `itemCount` is `0`, `0 && <span>...</span>` evaluates to `0` itself (that's what `&&` returns when its left side is falsy -- the falsy value, not `false`). React renders numbers as visible text content just like strings, so this produces a literal, visible "0" on the page -- exactly in the situation where you wanted nothing to show at all. `false`, `null`, and `undefined` all render as nothing, but `0` (and, similarly, `NaN`) render as themselves. The fix is to make sure the left-hand side is an actual boolean: `itemCount > 0 && <span>...</span>`, or `Boolean(itemCount) && ...`. This is worth checking any time a `&&` condition's left side could plausibly be a number rather than something already boolean.

To render one component per item in an array, `.map()` each item into a JSX element and let JSX render the resulting array directly -- React accepts an array of elements as a valid child just as readily as a single element:

```jsx
function TodoList({ todos }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {todos.map((todo) => (
        <li key={todo.id}>{todo.text}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

This is the standard way to turn any array of data into UI -- no separate 'list rendering' API exists; it's the same `.map()` you'd use for any other array transformation, just returning JSX instead of, say, numbers or strings.

examples.code:
function CartBadge({ itemCount }) {
  // BUGGY: renders a literal "0" when itemCount is 0
  return <div>{itemCount && <span className="badge">{itemCount}</span>}</div>;
}

function FixedCartBadge({ itemCount }) {
  // FIXED: the left side of && is now an actual boolean
  return <div>{itemCount > 0 && <span className="badge">{itemCount}</span>}</div>;
}

// BUGGY: key={index} lets checkbox state 'stick' to a position, not a player
function PlayerList({ players, onSort }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={onSort}>Sort alphabetically</button>
      <ul>
        {players.map((player, index) => (
          <li key={index}>
            {player.name} <input type="checkbox" defaultChecked={player.active} />
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
}

// FIXED: key={player.id} follows the actual player, not the list position
function FixedPlayerList({ players, onSort }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={onSort}>Sort alphabetically</button>
      <ul>
        {players.map((player) => (
          <li key={player.id}>
            {player.name} <input type="checkbox" defaultChecked={player.active} />
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
}

